Exact 2013 Li2 a/c spectroscopy reproduction
Prepared on 16 October 2026 from the preserved 18 September 2026 benchmark.
This package contains the exact input, observations, source, and saved output
of the corrected calculation. Packaging did not compile the source or rerun
the fit. No binary is included.
Result and what was fitted
The saved corrected calculation gives dimensionless RMS deviation (DRMSD)
0.79113699 for 2,798 observations, compared with Nike's archived
0.791111. The important source correction restored the historical
retardation in the c-state long-range interaction, with consistent
derivatives. It did not require changing the numerical input or adding fit
parameters. Current lithium masses were retained; a separate historical-code
test found that changing to these masses shifts the final DRMSD by about
0.000027.
This benchmark simultaneously fits the a-state and c-state spectroscopy,
including c-to-a transition frequencies and independently fitted upper-level
energies. It is not an a-state binding-energy-only fit and does not include
the proposed new-data-only staged comparison.
There are 39 freely fitted quantities: 19 physical potential coefficients and
20 independent upper-level energies. The physical coefficients comprise four
a-state beta coefficients (polynomial degree 3), one a-state adiabatic BOB
coefficient, ten c-state beta coefficients (degree 9), and four c-state
adiabatic BOB coefficients. BOB means Born–Oppenheimer breakdown. Well depths,
equilibrium distances and supplied long-range constants are fixed in this
benchmark. The input uses a-state reference distance 8.0 angstroms and c-state
reference distance 4.8 angstroms.
Which DRMSD numbers to compare
Implementation	All observations: 2,798	After singleton count correction: 2,796
Earlier unretarded result sent previously	0.80882507	0.80911430
Corrected result supplied here	0.79113699	0.79141989
Nike's archived result	0.791111	approximately 0.79139389
For observation i, let c_i be its calculated value, f_i its observed value,
and u_i its effective uncertainty, all in the same units. Each dimensionless
residual is (c_i - f_i)/u_i. First square each residual separately, then add:
    Q = ((c_1-f_1)/u_1)^2 + ((c_2-f_2)/u_2)^2 + ...
DRMSD = sqrt(Q/N)

Here N is the number of accepted observations. DRMSD does not divide by the
number of observations minus the number of fitted parameters.
Two independent upper-level energies each occur in just one transition, so
each can absorb its only observation without constraining the potentials.
The program's final count correction removes these two observations and two
parameters. With the same squared-residual sum Q, the separate construction is
    DRMSD_corrected = sqrt(Q/(2798-2))
= sqrt(Q/2798) * sqrt(2798/2796).

Thus the 39-parameter/2,798-observation and 37-parameter/2,796-observation
summary lines describe the same fitted result with different counting.
The previously quoted 0.809 is the latter convention for the old result;
compare like conventions when checking agreement.
Files and exact input
`input.5`: exact corrected-benchmark input.
`DATA\_c-a\_05-04-13.4`: exact corresponding observations.
`source/1-s2.0-S0022407316301078-mmc2.f`: exact amalgamated source used
for the saved corrected result; no INCLUDE or external module dependencies
were found in this source. Do not add the historical standalone AFdiag.f.
`expected/`: saved full `.6` report, `.7` record, summary and run log.
`provenance/`: original source/run checksums, original Nike input,
compiler settings and the recorded build log.
`SHA256SUMS`: checksums of the files in this package.
The only difference between `input.5` and
`provenance/original\_nike\_input.5` is the observation-file path: the original
`../../DATA\_c-a\_05-04-13.4` is replaced with the colocated basename. Numerical
parameters, free/fixed flags, isotopologue order, grids, weights, and rounding
controls are unchanged.
The original shared output folder omitted the `.5`; this package supplies
it together with the matching source and observations. Earlier versions of
the program also had a compatibility defect when reading the mixed 2013
header conventions. This source includes that repair. The recipient's
specific gfortran failure cannot be diagnosed from a description alone;
compiler and runtime logs would distinguish it from a missing data path or
a source-version mismatch.
Source and compiler provenance
Exact source SHA-256:
    527925961e4ed09ddb70681c3ef579525cfcb78c514801c725faca8b70eaad19

The original benchmark executable (not included) had SHA-256:
    907c9ebb001f3cb29f77189c24f06e0fe9a96e319d81d90cb853bebd55a69b1f

The build used GNU Fortran 16.2.0 on Apple silicon with:
    gfortran -O3 -mcpu=apple-m5 -flto=auto -funroll-loops

The recorded build used separate compilation and linking through the root
Makefile, with no fast-math flag. The compiler and build record contains the
original macOS SDK and deployment settings. These machine-specific settings
are provenance, not requirements for a different computer. The source is
fixed-form Fortran. This package has not been rebuilt or tested on a different
compiler or operating system, and exact last-digit agreement is not promised.
The source was recovered from the preserved `before\_vmax\_fix` snapshot and
its SHA-256 checked against the source checksum recorded with this triplet
result. Later standard-source reader changes are deliberately not substituted
for the source that generated the enclosed `.6`.
If repeating the calculation later
Compile the supplied source for your own platform using your local build
settings. Then use a new working directory containing copies of `input.5`
and `DATA\_c-a\_05-04-13.4`; run the resulting executable with standard input
redirected from `input.5`, for example:
    /absolute/path/to/compiled/pot < input.5 > run.log 2>&1

This command is documentation only; it was not executed while preparing this
package. Keep generated results in that fresh directory. The program writes
outputs using the basename in `input.5`, so the supplied expected results are
kept separately under `expected/` to prevent accidental replacement.
Verify completion, the expected observation count and both final fit-summary
lines before comparing numbers. Preserve any failed-build or failed-run log.
